# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows clone this repo is developed from, so
# text files are checked out with CRLF by default. That is fine for HTML and
# CSS and fatal for shell scripts: a script whose shebang line ends in CRLF
# fails to execute at all, with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`. Git hooks are
# the case that matters here — a hook that cannot run is a hook that silently
# stops enforcing anything.
#
# Pin every shell script to LF regardless of platform or local git config.
hooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
